@*
    A task's detail pane: the groups of rows that say when it happens, how it
    ranks, and what hangs off it.

    Two columns, because these rows are short and the panel they sit in is not.
    Every row in one stack means reading past the whole of "when" to find out how
    important the thing is, and it pushes the body — the part somebody opened the
    panel to write — below the fold on a laptop. Side by side, the pane is one
    glance.

    The columns balance rather than being placed, which is the decision worth
    arguing here. A grid puts a third group under the first one and leaves a hole
    beside it, so a pane of one tall group and two short ones comes out a row
    taller than anything in it. Columns fill instead: the short groups stack under
    one another in the second column, and the pane is as tall as its longest
    column. That is vertical space the body gets back on every task.

    How many columns there are is the width's answer and not a number written down
    here, so the same pane is a single column when there is no room for a second
    one. That is a requirement rather than a nicety: the mobile channel is a single
    column by design, and a pane that insisted on two would give it two unreadable
    ones. The column width is also what stops a third one appearing inside a panel
    of the width this is drawn at — three subjects abreast would be a row of
    captions, not a pane.

    Above the columns there is room for one row on its own, across the full width.
    That is not a slot for anything that would not fit: it is for the row that is a
    decision rather than a fact — "put this in today" — and it is one row because
    two things at the top of a pane is a pane with nothing at the top of it.

    Below them there is room for the other kind of row the columns are wrong for:
    the one whose value is not a word. A column is about sixteen rem wide, which is
    enough for "Friday" and "medium" and not enough for a list of other tasks — a
    dependency picker in a column wraps its chips one per line and turns a row into
    a paragraph. Full width is what that row actually needs, and putting it under
    the columns rather than in them also keeps it out of the balancing, so the two
    columns still end level.

    Nothing here says which groups a pane has, or what goes in the lead. What a
    task can be set to is the host's answer, the same way it is for every other
    component in this folder.

    The rows line up across the gap, which is a rule about height rather than about
    layout: every row reserves the line its value would take whether or not it has
    one, and the groups sit on one rhythm rather than being spaced apart from each
    other. Without both, "Priority" in the second column lands between "Remind me"
    and "Add due date" in the first, and a pane whose rows are at eleven different
    heights reads as a pile rather than as two columns.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId">
    @if (Lead is not null)
    {
        @* Above the columns rather than in them, so it spans the pane whatever the
           width has decided the columns are. *@
        <div class="task-action-pane__lead">@Lead</div>
    }

    @* The columns belong to this box and not to the pane itself, because the lead
       row must not be in them: a row that flowed with the groups would be balanced
       along with them and end up as the top of one column rather than across both. *@
    <div class="task-action-pane__columns">@Groups</div>

    @if (Trailing is not null)
    {
        <div class="task-action-pane__trailing">@Trailing</div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The one row that goes across the top, when there is one. Left out
    /// entirely when there is not, so a pane without one starts at its first
    /// group.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Lead { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The groups, in reading order. <see cref="TaskActionGroup"/> in
    /// practice: a pane of ungrouped rows would be the stack this exists to stop
    /// being.
    /// <para>
    /// Named rather than taken as child content, which it has to be once there are
    /// two slots — and which is the better read anyway: a pane declares a lead and
    /// its groups, rather than a lead and whatever else was lying around.
    /// </para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Groups { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The groups that go under the columns, across the full width, when
    /// there are any. Left out entirely when there are not.
    /// <para>
    /// For the group whose rows need the width rather than the column — a
    /// dependency picker, a list of other tasks — not for whatever did not fit.
    /// A row put here that would have been happy in a column is a row the reader
    /// has to look for in a third place.
    /// </para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Trailing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Lays the groups out as one band across the width instead of balanced
    /// columns.
    /// <para>
    /// The same pane in a different room. Everything above describes a panel
    /// docked beside a list — a column about sixteen rem wide, where a row that
    /// spends a line is cheap and the pane's height is the panel's height. A
    /// selection bar is the opposite shape: it is a strip across the top of a
    /// list, every line it takes is a line of the list it is covering, and it has
    /// a thousand-odd pixels of width sitting unused. Balanced columns there gave
    /// the backlog's bulk bar a resting height of three hundred and eighty pixels
    /// with the right half of it empty, because columns spread <em>groups</em>
    /// sideways and never the rows inside one.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// So the band flows the groups left to right and wraps only when the width
    /// runs out, and a group flows its own rows the same way. Two consequences are
    /// deliberate. A row is as tall as its label — the reserved line that keeps
    /// two columns level is a rule about columns, and there are none here. And a
    /// narrow container still stacks, because wrapping is the whole mechanism: the
    /// mobile channel gets the single column it needs without being told about it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <see cref="Lead"/> and <see cref="Trailing"/> still work and are still
    /// their own full-width bands. A host in a bar usually wants neither: a lead
    /// row across a strip is the strip, and a trailing group is the vertical space
    /// the band was chosen to avoid. Put the wide control in the group it belongs
    /// to and let it wrap.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Band { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    private string ClassValue => string.Join(' ', new[]
    {
        "task-action-pane",
        Band ? "task-action-pane--band" : null,
        CssClass
    }.Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part)));
}
